  <p>
    Most fence questions in St. George come down to one: do I need to get the city involved before I dig? The answer is in
    St. George City Code Title 10, Chapter 18, "Fences and Retaining Walls." This page translates the permit section into plain language
    and gives you a checklist. The wording of the code is quoted or closely paraphrased below, and the text was rechecked
    on September 26, 2026, when the code site showed it current through Ordinance 2026-068 (passed August 20, 2026).
  </p>

  <h2>What §10-18-5 says</h2>
  <div class="sgfw-permit">
    <p><strong>Retaining walls of four feet or more.</strong> Rockery, segmental block, reinforced concrete, or CMU retaining walls four feet (4') or greater in height shall not be constructed without first obtaining a permit from the city of St. George unless shown in detail on an approved site development plan.</p>
    <p class="mt-3"><strong>Anything in a front setback.</strong> A permit is required prior to construction for any fence or retaining wall being constructed in a front setback unless shown in detail on approved site development plans.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    Two separate triggers, then: <strong>location</strong> (any fence or retaining wall in a front setback, regardless of height) and
    <strong>size</strong> (retaining walls of the listed types at 4 feet or greater, wherever they are). A fence in a side or rear yard is not
    covered by the front-setback trigger, but it still has to meet the height limits in §10-18-1, and you should confirm your particular
    lot with the city.
  </p>

  <h2>How the city measures height</h2>
  <p>
    Height determines whether the size trigger applies, and the code measures differently depending on the wall type:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Concrete and CMU retaining walls</strong> are measured from the top of the footing to the top of the wall.</li>
    <li><strong>Rockery and segmental block retaining walls</strong> are measured from the exposed face to finished grade adjacent to the base of the wall.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That difference matters. A CMU wall that shows 3 feet above the soil can measure taller once the footing is counted,
    so do not rely on a rough visual guess.
  </p>

  <h2>Fence heights you still have to meet (§10-18-1)</h2>
  <p>
    Even where no permit is required, the fence must meet the height rules. In most zones, no fence may exceed 4 feet in the
    front yard, and no fence may exceed 6 feet 4 inches in side and rear yards. Within 10 feet behind the sidewalk, only decorative
    fences with at least 50 percent of the fence open, such as picket or wrought iron, are permitted. Some zones (RE-37.5, RE-5, A-1,
    A-5, A-10, and R-1-40) allow up to 6 feet 4 inches in the front setback if the fence is at least 50 percent open and stays at
    least 10 feet from the sidewalk or property line. Barbed wire is prohibited in residential zones except for agricultural use in
    residential estates.
  </p>

  <h2>Step by step: does your project need a permit?</h2>
  <ol>
    {steps.map((s) => (
      <li><strong>{s.name}.</strong> {s.text}</li>
