  </ol>

  <h2>What to submit</h2>
  <p>
    According to §10-18-5, the application must include a site plan showing property lines, sidewalks, buildings, height, and
    the locations of all fences and retaining walls, plus a landscape and drainage plan for retaining walls. If a company is
    building the project, ask whether they prepare those drawings and whether the cost is in the quote.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to apply and who to call</h2>
  <p>
    The City of St. George Building Department is at 61 S Main St, St. George, UT 84770, open Monday through Friday from 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.
    (435-627-4100), and accepts permit submissions through the city's CityInspect portal. Contact details were checked on
    September 26, 2026; confirm them before you go.
  </p>

  <h2>Permit mistakes to avoid</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Assuming a fence in the front yard is fine because it is only 3 feet tall. In a front setback, height does not matter for the permit trigger.</li>
    <li>Assuming a contractor's "we never need a permit" is right without checking your lot.</li>
    <li>Building a retaining wall to a height that "looks" under 4 feet without measuring by the city's method.</li>
    <li>Skipping the drainage plan on a retaining wall.</li>
    <li>Getting HOA approval and thinking the city is covered, or the reverse. They are separate.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sgfw-note">
